023 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "27bbff50-cd94-5a57-a122-63a5a9e1f48a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151024 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151024 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151024 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "748da8c9-662a-5392-a91c-60cb55261c43" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186151254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186151255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186151256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186151257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151253 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186151258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151253 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151253 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151253 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "843cb01e-6e15-5891-b40c-15d746118c24" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151254 #edb/ref 1080 "-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151254 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151254 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b5feeed0-c4f8-5820-a07d-5677ccdba2d6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151255 #edb/ref 1080 "2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151255 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151255 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ba18f0e2-3201-541f-8285-f513a5a67de3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151256 #edb/ref 1080 "1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151256 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151256 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "f648a5ad-a6a5-574d-a3c3-2b4a49a40053" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151257 #edb/ref 1080 "-2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151257 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151257 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "40923ad8-0e07-598d-8012-7b3e76ffc60d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151258 #edb/ref 1080 "3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151258 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151258 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7c62cc53-e991-5c81-918e-c3b85d64dd95" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151954 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6496" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151954 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $36u + 19v = \\text{gcd}(36,19)$. What is the value of $u+v$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151954 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "df4647c2-73f7-57eb-b013-68d7f170266a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151954 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n36 &= \\begin{vmatrix}19 \\cdot 1 & +\\end{vmatrix}17 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 19 &= \\end{vmatrix}17 \\cdot 1\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 2 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 17|= & 2 \\cdot 8\\end{vmatrix}+ \\mid 1\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(19,36) =1\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, up to (and including) the equation where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n17 &= 36 - 19 \\cdot 1 \\\\\n2 &= 19 - 17 \\\\\n1 &= 17 - 2 \\cdot 8\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n1 &= 17 - 2 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 17 - (19 - 17) \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 17 \\cdot 9 - 19 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= [36 - 19] \\cdot 9 - 19 \\cdot 8 \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 9 - 19 \\cdot 17 \\\\\n&= 36 \\cdot 9 + 19 \\cdot (-17)\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=9,v=-17$, and we can write\n\n$$\n1 = 36\\cdot \\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{9} + 19\\cdot (\\bbox[2pt, lightgray]{-17})\n$$\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nu+v=9+(-17)=-8\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151981 #edb/ref 1069 "e-6763" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151981 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $30u + 8v = \\text{gcd}(30,8)$. What is the value of $u+v$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151981 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed22bd9f-4dc9-528f-890f-2a81c4d353cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151981 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n30 &= \\begin{vmatrix}8 \\cdot 3 & +\\end{vmatrix}6 \\\\\n↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 8 &= \\end{vmatrix}6 \\cdot 1\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 2 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 6|= & 2 \\cdot 3\\end{vmatrix}+ \\mid 0\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result,\n\n$$\n\\text{gcd}(30,8) =2\n$$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, up to (and including) the equation where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n6 &= 30 - 8 \\cdot 3 \\\\\n2 &= 8 - 6\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n2 &= 8 - 6 \\\\\n&= 8 - (30 - 8 \\cdot 3) \\\\\n&= 8 \\cdot 4 - 30 \\\\\n&= 30 \\cdot (-1) + 8 \\cdot 4\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=-1$ and $v=4$.\n\nFinally,\n\n$$\nu+v=(-1)+4=3\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152752 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152163 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152768 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152163 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Coprime" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152163 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151954 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152163 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "b6c5f369-1196-5ace-9450-815a4ee2cc8f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152184 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152184 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152769 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152184 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186152791 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152184 #edb/ref 1039 "Applying the Extended Euclidean Algorithm When the Two Numbers are Not Coprime" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152184 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186151981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152184 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "4caf9fc6-9247-5807-84de-208b731116e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152344 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152344 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152344 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152344 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186153099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152344 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152344 #edb/ref 1045 "The Extended Euclidean Algorithm" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152344 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186153071 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152344 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186049094 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152344 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "79d375df-4e87-5b44-81dc-23c2a98b6b63" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152752 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151019 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152752 #edb/ref 1069 "q-76861" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152752 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $43u + 17v = gcd(43, 17)$. What is the value of $u + v$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152752 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ed1e5e71-2e8a-56a4-872d-0a29ad0e0a2a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152791 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186151253 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152791 #edb/ref 1069 "q-78470" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152791 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $28u + 16v = gcd(28, 16)$. What is the value of $u + v$?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152791 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "29de6c34-4477-5dd6-9882-390e0598c400" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153027 #edb/ref 1061 183906 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153027 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7e080049-7b49-599a-b9dd-9f6cd03fa661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153027 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153065 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153027 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153065 #edb/ref 1061 23950 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153065 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a1c1eb96-adce-53a7-848d-bf42b0fb27f8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153065 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152163 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153071 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153099 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153071 #edb/ref 1061 24052 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153071 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0dca946f-169d-57e2-88ac-167fa4ed7275" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153071 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186153360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153099 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186153065 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153099 #edb/ref 1061 24976 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153099 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "85cccd96-f97c-5a7c-b7a5-e3abb690a42e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153099 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186152184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153360 #edb/ref 1064 "For any two integers $a$ and $b$, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nThis is known as **Bézout's identity**.\n\nTo find the integers $u$ and $v$, we can use the **extended Euclidean algorithm**, which involves the following three steps:\n\n1. Carry out the Euclidean algorithm to find $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$.\n2. Solve for the remainder at each step, up to (and including) the step where the greatest common divisor appeared as a remainder.\n3. Back-substitute the remainders to reach the equation $au+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$.\n\nFor example, let's use the extended Euclidean algorithm to find integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\n48u+18v= \\textrm{gcd}(48,18)\n$$\n\n- **Step 1:** First, we carry out the Euclidean algorithm to find $\\textrm{gcd}(48,18)$.\n$48|=\\begin{vmatrix}18 \\cdot 2 & +\\end{vmatrix}12; ↙\\begin{vmatrix}↙ \\\\ 18|=\\end{vmatrix}12 \\cdot 1\\begin{vmatrix}+ & 6 \\\\ ↙ & ↙ \\\\ 12|= & 6 \\cdot 2\\end{vmatrix}+ \\mid 0$\nSo, we find that $\\text{gcd}(48,18) =6$.\n- **Step 2:** We solve for the remainder (the rightmost term) at each step, up to (and including) the step where the greatest common divisor appeared as an integer.\n$12|= 48 - 18 \\cdot 2; 6|= 18 - 12$\n- **Step 3:** Finally, we back-substitute the remainders to reach the equation $48u+18v= 6$.\n$6|= 18 - 12; = 18 - (48 - 18 \\cdot 2); = 18 - 48 + 18 \\cdot 2; = (18 + 18 \\cdot 2) - 48; = 18 \\cdot 3 - 48; = 48 \\cdot (-1) + 18 \\cdot (3)$\n\nTherefore, $u=-1$ and $v=3$." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153360 #edb/ref 1065 4024 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153360 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "6f364b85-4d93-5a09-a174-dea640e1c448" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153360 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153483 #edb/ref 1064 "Throughout this lesson we've made use of **Bézout's identity**, which states that for any two integers $a$ and $b$, there exist integers $u$ and $v$ such that\n\n$$\nau+bv= \\textrm{gcd}(a,b)\n$$\n\nIt can also be shown that any integer combination of $a$ and $b$ is also a multiple of $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$. In other words, for *any* integers $u'$ and $v'$, we have\n\n$$\n\\textrm{gcd}(a,b) \\mid (au'+bv')\n$$\n\nMoreover, the set of integers\n$\\big\\{au'+bv' \\,:\\, u', v' \\in\\mathbb Z \\big \\}$\ncontains *all* the multiples of $\\textrm{gcd}(a,b)$.\n\nWe'll make use of these results in future lessons." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153483 #edb/ref 1065 9653 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153483 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9505be6e-ec53-5537-bf81-d4b7b197c384" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186153483 #edb/ref 1067 "Final Remarks" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
